      "content": "Without modern banking infrastructure, merchants relied on a sophisticated **\"singular system\" of credit** built on strict written contracts and mutual reputation among established trading families who would guarantee each other's debts across borders. They managed risk by using **secrecy and compartmentalization**, ensuring that stolen letters of credit could not be easily counterfeited by allowing each merchant to hold only a fragmented share of the accounts. Furthermore, they utilized **standardized local storage facilities** in major city-states to mitigate inventory risk, often leaving goods in the care of trusted partners who issued guaranteed receipts for safekeeping.",
